---
import SiteLayout from '../../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const href = (value: string) => `${base.replace(/\/?$/, '/')}${value.replace(/^\/+/, '')}`;
const flow = 'Sandbox platform\n' +
  '├── ConnectionAgent Domain  ·  Agent / Runtime / Skills\n' +
  '├── Tool Plane              ·  discovery / gateway / common capabilities\n' +
  '├── Environment Plane       ·  env-core / simulated & real worlds / env-fabric\n' +
  '├── Evaluation              ·  bench-suite / bench-engine\n' +
  '├── Control / Surface       ·  control / console\n' +
  '└── Shared Foundation       ·  nodus\n' +
  '\nPlatform-level composition and compatibility governance.\n' +
  'Each component retains its own contract and fact authority.';
---
<SiteLayout title="Sandbox Platform Architecture" lang="en" description="Sandbox platform layers, component responsibilities, and cross-repository authority boundaries.">
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Sandbox / Architecture</span>
    <h1>Sandbox platform architecture</h1>
    <p class="lede">Sandbox is the product-level composition and governance layer for Agents, tools, environments, and evaluation. It defines how components work together, remain compatible, and form runnable experiments. Each component retains ownership of its implementation, contracts, and domain facts.</p>\n    <p><a href={href('/components/sandbox/')}>Read the detailed architecture and responsibility boundaries maintained by the owner repository →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section"><div class="grid"><div class="card wide">
    <h3>Platform layers</h3><pre><code>{flow}</code></pre>
    <p class="muted">This is the target architecture, not a claim that every component migration or end-to-end acceptance is complete. Current repository names may differ from target names; consult Sandbox migration records and owner repositories for status.</p>
  </div></div></section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Responsibilities by plane</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Plane</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Main components (target taxonomy)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>ConnectionAgent Domain</td><td>Agent Hosts, planning and routing, Skill source/build, governed execution, Outcome, Reconcile, and Recovery.</td><td><code>ca-agent</code>, <code>ca-runtime</code>, <code>ca-dsh</code>, <code>ca-nanobot</code>, <code>ca-skills</code>, <code>ca-skill-toolchain</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tool Plane</td><td>Tool discovery and invocation protocols, gateway mapping, and cross-environment capabilities. The gateway does not own underlying business effects.</td><td><code>tool-mcp-gw</code>, <code>tool-common</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Environment Plane</td><td>Environment substrate, simulated or real worlds, authoritative source facts, external-system integration, network observation, and controlled intervention.</td><td><code>env-core</code>, <code>env-5gc-sim</code>, <code>env-5gc-real</code>, <code>env-fabric</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Evaluation assets, task freezing, execution attempts, evidence validity, Verdicts, and result analysis.</td><td><code>bench-suite</code>, <code>bench-engine</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Control / Surface</td><td>Select and compose resources, manage outer lifecycle and experiment runs, and provide the human-facing interface.</td><td><code>control</code>, <code>console</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shared Foundation</td><td>Reusable domain-neutral mechanics without taking over domain semantics.</td><td><code>nodus</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Composition authority is not fact authority</h2>
    <p>Sandbox can register components, pin compatible versions, define compositions, and enforce system-level integration gates. That does not make the platform repository the source of every fact.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>ca-runtime</code> owns Runtime Outcomes for governed execution; Sandbox must not create a second writer.</li>
      <li>Environment owners own their world state and source facts; Sandbox does not replace them with a central state store.</li>
      <li>The Benchmark Engine forms Verdicts from frozen tasks and evidence rules; Sandbox does not adjudicate the same trial separately.</li>
      <li>Control manages outer resource and experiment lifecycle; that does not give it ownership of Environment state or Benchmark semantics.</li>
      <li>Console presents and operates platform capabilities but is not authoritative for underlying facts.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Environments and the system under test</h2>
    <p>Sandbox can compose simulated, real, and hybrid environments. An environment is where the system under test runs and is observed; it is not synonymous with a single simulator. The frozen EvaluationTask declares whether a trial includes the Agent, Runtime, tools, or an end-to-end composition. Repository names do not determine the test boundary.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="real-5gc-workflow">
    <p><a href="/en/runbooks/real-5gc/">Run and validate this workflow: Real 5GC runbook →</a></p><p><a href="/en/interfaces/real-5gc-evidence/">Interfaces and evidence: contracts, authority and independent verdicts →</a></p>
    <h2>How a real 5GC request moves through Sandbox</h2>
    <p>The current Open5GS + UERANSIM integration makes the boundaries concrete. Keep the validated R1 observation path separate from the R2-B Agent-governed path, whose overall closeout is still blocked.</p>
    <h3>R1: observe and forward SBI without taking over N2</h3>
    <pre><code>UERANSIM UE
  -> gNB
  -> Open5GS AMF
  -> env-fabric (observe and transparently forward SBI)
  -> SCP
  -> Open5GS NF (for example, SMF)

N2 / NGAP: direct gNB -> AMF
SBI: traverses env-fabric and the mandatory SCP in the current topology</code></pre>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What the component does</th><th>What supports the next step</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>1. UE registration and session request</td><td>UERANSIM, gNB, and Open5GS use their native state machines for the radio-side flow. Fabric does not intercept SCTP/N2 or parse NGAP.</td><td>The SBI request produced by AMF, not a NAS state inferred by Fabric.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. SBI observation</td><td><code>env-fabric</code> retains protocol provenance and observes SBI requests. R1 neither publishes NetworkIntent nor blocks the request.</td><td>The source request, boundary, and correlatable observation record.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. NF communication</td><td>The request reaches the target NF through the required SCP while Fabric forwards it transparently.</td><td>The actual request/response path and Open5GS-owned state.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4. Verify the result</td><td>Check registration, PDU Session, PFCP, TUN, and user-plane connectivity from environment-side evidence. Forwarding a request does not prove that a session was established.</td><td>Owner-authoritative state and an actual user-plane test.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p>The recorded R0/R1 baseline includes NRF/SCP startup, UE registration, PDU Session establishment, PFCP, and UE user-plane ping 3/3 PASS. These are recorded integration results, not proof that every R2-B closeout gate has passed.</p>
    <h3>R2-B: add Agent governance at an explicit boundary</h3>
    <pre><code>AMF -- CreateSMContext / N1 SM --> env-fabric
                                      |
                                      | retain source material
                                      | extract whitelisted fields
                                      | create exact actionRef
                                      | PREPARED -> HELD
                                      v
                              NetworkIntent v2
                                      |
                                      v
                         ca-agent-demo / Runtime
                                      |
                              Predicate admission
                                      |
                         execute exact actionRef only after admission
                                      |
                                      v
                              env-fabric -> SCP -> SMF</code></pre>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Boundary</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Shortcuts that are not allowed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>env-fabric</code></td><td>Retains original SBI material, extracts authoritative fields, produces NetworkIntent v2, and manages an exact held action with a deadline.</td><td>No N2/NGAP handling, no full NAS decoding, and no execution of an action before it is published and held.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>connectionagent</code></td><td>Owns the canonical NetworkIntent schema; v2 is current for R2-B and v1 remains historical.</td><td>Do not create a competing copy of the schema in the Sandbox documentation repository.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ca-agent-demo</code></td><td>Consumes NetworkIntent, projects it into Skill/Runtime context, and performs admission through the Runtime PredicateEngine.</td><td>Do not query Open5GS or UERANSIM directly to bypass Environment/Observation boundaries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sandbox</td><td>Owns cross-repository composition, dependency ordering, joined acceptance, and evidence closeout.</td><td>Does not replace component ownership of domain facts, Outcomes, or contracts.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <p><strong>Current status:</strong> M5 live governed-action/user-plane evidence and the separate M6 R1 regression evidence are recorded as PASS. Overall R2-B closeout remains BLOCKED on owner PR-range diff-check and evidence-hygiene gates. Do not describe R2-B as fully complete.</p>
    <p>Canonical details: <a href="https://github.com/odango-chan/sandbox/blob/main/docs/design/real-5gc-agent-control.md">Real 5GC Agent-Controlled Environment</a> · <a href="https://github.com/odango-chan/sandbox/blob/main/docs/design/r2b-joint-gap-audit.md">R2-B joint gap audit</a>.</p>
  </section>

